bluetooth: do not drain cn_WaitReqs in place -- it hangs the machine

bConnUp() re-dispatches the requests a class queued while the link was down
by draining cn_WaitReqs with RemHead() and calling bConnHandleRequest() on
each. That function puts a request straight back on cn_WaitReqs whenever the
GATT client is busy (hwconn.c, BEPT_GATT_CHAR: `if(cn->cn_CtrlReq ||
cn->cn_CCCDBusy || (cn->cn_EnumState != ENUM_IDLE)) bReqQueueAdd(...)`), so
RemHead() takes it off and the body puts it back. The loop never terminates,
and nothing inside it can terminate it either: clearing cn_CtrlReq needs the
GATT connect to complete, which needs the very task that is spinning.

It only bites on a *reused* connection object, which is why it looks
intermittent. The advert-driven path (bConnAdvertising with cn == NULL, the
one that logs "is awake - reconnecting") allocates a fresh connection whose
queue is empty, so the loop does not run at all. The path where a class was
already waiting (cn_WaitAdv, logged as "is awake - connecting") arrives with
every read the class re-issued meanwhile -- bthid retries every 250 ms -- and
the second of those closes the loop.

Measured on a Raspberry Pi 3 on 2026-08-26 with a bonded LE mouse switching
away and back. A detached heartbeat writing to the card and to DEBUG: every
second stops dead between its Echo and its Date, immediately after

    Connected to BT 5.0 Mouse (LE link, handle 0040, central).

and before

    BT 5.0 Mouse: encrypting the link with the stored LE key.

-- the two lines that bracket this loop. Runs that reconnect through the
"reconnecting" branch complete normally in the same session.

The fix is already in the file, twenty lines above: bDispatchWaiting()
detaches the whole queue onto a local list before walking it, so a re-queued
request lands on a now-empty cn_WaitReqs and is handled on the next event
instead of re-entering the loop. bConnUp() gets the same treatment.

See AI_context/issues/ISSUE-0062.md.

